this week we have a great video question  from reader Robin Kaiser when travelling  in Europe can I text people in America  without incurring charges and if not  what kind of arrangements do I need to  make Robin your vacation sounds like a  lot of fun what's not fun are the  charges you could come home to if you  start text messaging when you're away  carriers can charge an arm and a leg for  international service even for those  short texts but before you leave if you  sign up for one of their international  texting plans or at least one of the  bundled plans that includes  international texting you could save a  lot of money  check your carrier's websites for  international options and read the fine  print many of those carrier plans are  just for texting and don't include data  charges you'd incur if you were using  other apps and unless you sign up for an  international data package those data  overages could crush you that's why I  suggest turning off data just use  messaging apps like whatsapp or kick to  chat and share photos over Wi-Fi apps  like viber and Skype even let you make  calls over Wi-Fi and most hotels have  wireless and same with most coffee shops  you can turn off data on your iPhone by  going to settings then to the cellular  switch and then switching off cellular  data and LTE Android users can turn off  data by going to settings then data  usage and switching off mobile data of  course we're not going on vacation we're  always here so send us your tech  questions on Twitter and Facebook with  the hashtag SW SJD or email them to us  at personal tech at wsj.com
 

